# Vault policy

## Gate

Use when the user asks to author, review, or tighten a Vault ACL policy
(HCL/JSON policy document or capability list on a path). Skip when the
ask is only a KV/PKI data operation with no policy change. Always load
`vault-refuse-leak`.

Never invent policy names or path prefixes. Tie capabilities to mounts
and paths the user named or that MCP listed this turn.

## MCP coverage

Vault MCP does not currently expose policy write/read tools. Author
policy documents in-repo or via the operator’s approved Vault CLI/API
when asked. Use MCP `list_mounts` / `list_secrets` to **verify path
prefixes** exist before granting capabilities on them.

## Defaults

1. Least privilege: grant only the capabilities required (`read`, `list`,
   `create`, `update`, `delete`, `sudo` sparingly).
2. Prefer exact path prefixes under a known mount — avoid `*` at the
   mount root unless the user explicitly wants a broad admin policy.
3. Separate policies for humans, CI, and workloads when trust differs.
4. KV v2: remember data lives under `…/data/…` and metadata under
   `…/metadata/…` — grant only the segments the consumer needs.
5. PKI: issue/sign capabilities are powerful — scope to the specific
   role path when possible (`pki/issue/<role>`), not the whole mount.
6. Policy name should describe the consumer (`app-foo-read`,
   `ci-pki-issue`) — not `admin` or `all` unless it truly is.

## Capability guide

| Intent | Typical capabilities | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sync agent read | `read` (+ `list` if browsing) | No `create`/`update`/`delete` |
| App rotate own secret | `read` + `update` on one path | Avoid mount-wide `delete` |
| PKI issue | `create`/`update` on issue path | Not root CA generate |
| Mount admin | `sudo` + broad paths | Explicit admin ask only |

## Review checklist

1. Every path prefix maps to a real mount/path the user owns.
2. No accidental `sudo` or `+` capability grab.
3. No `secret/*` style grant when a single app path was requested.
4. Deny-by-omission: capabilities not listed are denied — do not add
   "just in case" verbs.
5. Document which auth role attaches this policy (name only).

## Report shape

1. Policy name
2. Path → capability summary (no secret values)
3. Attached auth role name when known
4. Residual risks (broad globs, sudo) called out plainly

## Refuse

- Do not grant root-equivalent policy to apps or CI "temporarily".
- Do not paste live Vault tokens used to apply the policy.
- Do not delete or overwrite production policies without an explicit
  verb and named policy.
